findings.body:
**Fix the `in` check at Line 584: it hides live environments whose names match `Object.prototype` keys.**

`name in expEnvs` also checks the prototype chain. Take a live environment named `constructor`, `toString`, `valueOf` or `hasOwnProperty` that the settings file does not list. The check returns true, so no "present live but not in the settings file" drift line is emitted. `compareEnvironment` is never called for that environment, so its secrets and variables skip the allowlist.

The header (Lines 23-26) says GitHub auto-creates an environment on first use. The header also says an unlisted environment must not escape the secret allowlist. A workflow that names such an environment would hide it from this check.

Line 579 (`actEnvs[name]`) has the same prototype lookup for expected names. Use an own-property check in both places.

<details>
<summary>Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
   for (const name of Object.keys(expEnvs).sort(byString)) {
-    const live = actEnvs[name];
+    const live = Object.hasOwn(actEnvs, name) ? actEnvs[name] : undefined;
     if (live === undefined) drift.push(`environment ${name}: missing`);
     else compareEnvironment(name, expEnvs[name], live, drift, notices, c3Attested);
   }
   for (const name of Object.keys(actEnvs).sort(byString)) {
-    if (!(name in expEnvs))
+    if (!Object.hasOwn(expEnvs, name))
       drift.push(`environment ${name}: present live but not in the settings file`);
   }
```
</details>

Add a vitest case with a live environment named `constructor`.
